::ENG2_U06_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide heading best communicates a finding?{
=A. Many Students Remain on Campus After Dismissal#Correct. This heading states what the evidence shows.
~B. Survey#Incorrect. This names a source but not the finding.
~C. Slide 2#Incorrect. This gives position, not meaning.
~D. Random Facts#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Which feature best shows a comparison between two survey numbers?{
~A. A full paragraph in tiny print.#Incorrect. This is hard to read.
=B. A simple labeled bar chart.#Correct. A bar chart clearly compares quantities.
~C. A decorative image with no labels.#Incorrect. This does not clarify the numbers.
~D. Moving text on every word.#Incorrect. This distracts from meaning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which slide text is most concise?{
~A. There are a lot of students and many things happen after school and it all matters.#Incorrect. This is wordy.
~B. Stuff happens.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=C. 68 of 120 surveyed students remain on campus after dismissal.#Correct. This is concise and evidence-based.
~D. This slide contains information and other material.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide sequence is most logical?{
~A. Conclusion, random image, evidence, topic.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~B. Decoration, evidence, unrelated joke, claim.#Incorrect. This does not build understanding.
~C. Evidence only, with no reasoning.#Incorrect. The audience needs reasoning.
=D. Finding, evidence, reasoning, conclusion.#Correct. This sequence builds understanding.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Why should slides avoid full paragraphs of speech text?{
=A. Slides should support the speaker with key points, not replace the speaker.#Correct. The speaker explains while slides guide understanding.
~B. The audience should not see evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
~C. The speaker should avoid explaining anything.#Incorrect. The speaker should explain.
~D. Paragraphs are always more visual.#Incorrect. Paragraphs can overload slides.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which visual feature would distract from the evidence?{
~A. A labeled chart.#Incorrect. This supports evidence.
=B. A busy background that makes numbers hard to read.#Correct. This distracts from the evidence.
~C. A clear heading.#Incorrect. This supports understanding.
~D. A short evidence label.#Incorrect. This supports understanding.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] How does structure help a digital presentation?{
~A. It hides the main idea.#Incorrect. Structure should reveal the main idea.
~B. It makes all slides unrelated.#Incorrect. Structure connects slides.
=C. It guides the audience through ideas in a logical order.#Correct. Structure helps the audience follow.
~D. It replaces the need for evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which statement best explains reasoning on a slide?{
~A. Source A is a source.#Incorrect. This does not explain why evidence matters.
~B. The chart has bars.#Incorrect. This describes the feature only.
~C. The survey was taken.#Incorrect. This is not reasoning.
=D. Supervised study space turns waiting time into safer academic time.#Correct. This explains the meaning of the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide choice best supports audience understanding?{
=A. Use one main idea per slide with a clear heading.#Correct. This keeps slides readable and focused.
~B. Put all notes on one slide.#Incorrect. This overloads the audience.
~C. Use unclear labels.#Incorrect. Labels should clarify.
~D. Hide important numbers.#Incorrect. Evidence should be visible.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Which heading best shows text-feature awareness?{
~A. Stuff#Incorrect. This heading is vague.
=B. Survey Evidence Shows a Real After-School Need#Correct. This heading identifies what the slide proves.
~C. My Slide#Incorrect. This does not help the audience.
~D. Numbers Maybe#Incorrect. This is vague and uncertain.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which slide bullet is clearest?{
~A. Lots of people are there and some things happen.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. There are many points and the issue is something.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=C. 49 surveyed students usually wait in hallways or similar areas.#Correct. This is specific and clear.
~D. Stuff in places after school.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which feature best supports a reasoning slide?{
~A. A random photograph unrelated to the topic.#Incorrect. This distracts.
~B. A long copied paragraph of the speech.#Incorrect. This overloads the slide.
~C. A decorative animation on every bullet.#Incorrect. This distracts.
=D. A concise statement connecting evidence to the proposed study space.#Correct. Reasoning should connect evidence to the claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which choice best supports oral delivery with slides?{
=A. Put key phrases on slides and explain details aloud.#Correct. Slides support the speaker.
~B. Read crowded slides word for word.#Incorrect. This weakens oral delivery.
~C. Avoid explaining evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence needs explanation.
~D. Put no labels on visuals.#Incorrect. Labels help the audience.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide title is strongest for a conclusion?{
~A. Done#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. Study Space Supports Safety and Learning#Correct. This reinforces the message.
~C. Last Slide#Incorrect. This labels sequence, not meaning.
~D. The End Maybe#Incorrect. This is informal and uncertain.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Why are labels important on a chart?{
~A. Labels make the chart more confusing.#Incorrect. Good labels clarify.
~B. Labels replace all reasoning.#Incorrect. Reasoning is still needed.
=C. Labels tell the audience what the numbers represent.#Correct. Labels help viewers understand the data.
~D. Labels should hide the evidence.#Incorrect. Labels should reveal meaning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which text is too wordy for a slide?{
~A. 68 of 120 students remain after dismissal.#Incorrect. This is concise.
~B. Supervised space supports learning.#Incorrect. This is concise.
~C. Finding: students need organized waiting space.#Incorrect. This is concise.
=D. There are many different things that could be said about the way students are waiting in different places after school and it is important for several reasons.#Correct. This is too long and overloaded.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide choice best clarifies evidence?{
=A. A chart with clear labels and readable numbers.#Correct. This makes evidence easy to understand.
~B. Tiny text with no labels.#Incorrect. This makes evidence hard to read.
~C. A bright image unrelated to the topic.#Incorrect. This distracts.
~D. A blank slide where evidence should appear.#Incorrect. This hides evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker action should accompany an evidence slide?{
~A. Stay silent and point at the screen.#Incorrect. The speaker should explain.
=B. Explain what the evidence shows and why it matters.#Correct. Oral explanation connects evidence to reasoning.
~C. Read unrelated facts.#Incorrect. This distracts.
~D. Skip the slide quickly.#Incorrect. Evidence needs explanation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Which slide structure avoids overload?{
~A. Five unrelated ideas on one slide.#Incorrect. This overloads the audience.
~B. One paragraph plus three charts on one slide.#Incorrect. This is too crowded.
=C. One main idea with one supporting feature.#Correct. This is focused and readable.
~D. No heading and no labels.#Incorrect. This makes meaning unclear.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which statement best connects finding and reasoning?{
~A. The slide has colors.#Incorrect. This does not connect ideas.
~B. The chart is a chart.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~C. There are numbers.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=D. Because many students remain after dismissal, a supervised study space would address an existing need.#Correct. This connects evidence to reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which checklist best matches Lesson 4?{
=A. Clear heading, concise wording, useful visual feature, logical sequence, and audience clarity.#Correct. This matches digital presentation design.
~B. Crowded slides, random images, and no labels.#Incorrect. These choices distract.
~C. Full speech paragraphs on every slide.#Incorrect. Slides should be concise.
~D. Evidence without reasoning or conclusion.#Incorrect. The audience needs reasoning and closure.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Which slide feature works like a text feature?{
=A. A heading that previews the slide's main idea.#Correct. A heading guides the audience like a text feature.
~B. A hidden number.#Incorrect. Hidden information does not guide readers.
~C. A random animation.#Incorrect. This does not structure meaning.
~D. A confusing background.#Incorrect. This distracts.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which slide text has the clearest expository voice?{
~A. It is good and stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The thing helps.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=C. A supervised study space would provide students with a productive waiting location.#Correct. This is clear and explanatory.
~D. Nice place, maybe.#Incorrect. This is vague and uncertain.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which design decision best helps the audience understand the survey evidence?{
~A. Remove the survey numbers.#Incorrect. This hides evidence.
~B. Use a background that covers the numbers.#Incorrect. This distracts.
~C. Add unrelated clip art.#Incorrect. This does not clarify evidence.
=D. Show the numbers in a simple chart with labels.#Correct. This makes the evidence easier to understand.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] What is the main goal of digital presentation choices?{
=A. To improve audience understanding of findings, evidence, and reasoning.#Correct. Digital choices should serve communication.
~B. To decorate every slide as much as possible.#Incorrect. Decoration is not the main goal.
~C. To replace the speaker completely.#Incorrect. Slides support the speaker.
~D. To make evidence harder to read.#Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
}
